;;; Parse.mon --- A friendly introduction to parser combinators

:author "Laluxx"
:version "1.1.0"
:keywords "how-to parser combinator sequencing repetition diagnostics"

;;; Commentary:

 A parser turns input into either a value or a useful explanation of why no
 value could be produced. A parser combinator is simply a function that builds
 a larger parser from smaller parsers. Instead of writing one large procedural
 loop, we describe a grammar by composing reusable pieces.

 In Monad, the strict parser shape used by Text.Parser is:

   Int -> Int -> [Int] -> ParseReply a

 The first Int is a diagnostic code chosen by the grammar. The second Int is
 the current source offset. The [Int] value is the unconsumed byte input. On
 success, ParseReply carries a value of type =a=, the next offset, and whether
 input was consumed. On failure it carries structured diagnostic evidence and
 the same consumption fact.

 This tutorial grows a parser in three small steps:

  1. =letter-parser= recognizes one letter =a=.
  2. =letter-then-digit= sequences that parser with a decimal digit.
  3. =parse-digits= repeats the digit parser safely with an explicit budget.

 The consumption bit matters when grammars branch. An empty failure allows an
 alternative parser to run; a failure after consuming input is committed. This
 is how the library avoids silently backtracking over evidence that already
 belongs to the chosen grammar branch.

 Try the tutorial from the =how_to= directory with:

   monad test Parse.mon

 Every example below is executable and the tests at the bottom explain the
 behavior they protect. Changing an input byte is a good way to explore the
 diagnostic renderer without needing to understand the implementation first.

;;; Code:

import Text.Parser
import Data.Char

module HowTo.Parse
  [letter-a? decimal-digit?
   letter-parser decimal-digit-parser
   letter-then-digit parse-letter-digit parse-digits]


;;; Recognizing one token

define letter-a? :: Char -> Bool
  character | = 0x61    -> True
            | otherwise -> False


define decimal-digit? :: Char -> Bool
  character -> digit? character


define letter-parser :: Int -> Int -> [Int] -> ParseReply Int
  code offset bytes -> parser-satisfy letter-a? code offset bytes


define decimal-digit-parser :: Int -> Int -> [Int] -> ParseReply Int
  code offset bytes -> parser-satisfy decimal-digit? code offset bytes


;;; Sequencing parsers

define digit-after-letter :: Int -> Int -> Int -> [Int] -> ParseReply Int
  letter code offset bytes -> decimal-digit-parser code offset bytes


define letter-then-digit :: Int -> Int -> [Int] -> ParseReply Int
  code offset bytes ->
    parser-bind letter-parser digit-after-letter code offset bytes


define parse-letter-digit :: [Int] -> ParseReply Int
  bytes -> letter-then-digit 100 0 bytes


;;; Repeating safely

define parse-digits :: [Int] -> ParseReply [Int]
  bytes -> parser-many-up-to 64 102 (parser-satisfy digit?) 101 0 bytes


;;; Inspecting replies

define valid-input   :: [Int] ⌜0x61 0x37⌝
define invalid-input :: [Int] ⌜0x61 0x78⌝


tests
  assert-eq (reply-value-or 0 (parse-letter-digit valid-input)) 0x37 "Sequencing returns the value produced by the second parser"
  assert-eq (reply-consumed? (parse-letter-digit invalid-input)) True "Failure after recognizing the letter remains committed"
  assert-eq (reply-error-offset (parse-letter-digit invalid-input)) 1 "A failed digit points at the second input byte"
  assert-eq (count (reply-value-or [] (parse-digits ⌜0x31 0x32 0x33 0x78⌝))) 3 "Bounded repetition collects the decimal prefix"
  assert-eq (render-parser-error "input.txt" invalid-input (parse-letter-digit invalid-input)) "input.txt:1:2: error: expected token 100\n  |\n1 | ax\n  |  ^" "Structured failures render a source location and caret"
